A missing per-package LICENSE file therefore does not mean "unlicensed". It
means BSL 1.1. That direction is deliberate — it fails closed.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Apache License 2.0 — the parts that touch your database
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each of these directories contains its own LICENSE file (Apache 2.0), and that
file, not the repository root LICENSE, governs it:

    packages/contracts            claim shapes, sealing rules, Evidence Pack
    packages/connector-postgres   the read-only Postgres connection and scope
    packages/packs-layer-a        detection rules read from the catalogue
    packages/packs-layer-b        detection rules read by sampling data
    packages/store                the local scan history and the diff
    packages/test-fixtures        test-only helpers; never published to npm
    apps/cli                      the command-line entry points

They are Apache 2.0 for two reasons, and the second is not a courtesy.

First, this product reads production databases. Anything that touches customer
data has to be auditable by the people whose data it is, so the connector, the
query templates, the redaction path and the detection rules can never move to a
closed tier — no matter what else does.

Second, the BSL Additional Use Grant permits redistribution of the Licensed
Work only *unmodified*. Under that constraint a contributor could not write a
new connector or a new rule pack without immediately falling outside the grant.
Putting those packages under Apache 2.0 is what makes outside contribution
possible at all.

